It's been a whirlwind 48 hours for Robert Pattinson .

The Hollywood Reporter heard from sources that the British star only found out he'd been tapped to succeed Daniel Craig as James Bond on Sunday morning, when Denis Villeneuve &#x2014;&#xA0;who's helming the next Bond movie &#x2014;&#xA0;called him. Producer Amy Pascal was said to inform the other candidates.

Within a day, the whole world knew too. Amazon MGM Studios, which bought the creative rights to the Bond franchise from producer siblings Michael G. Wilson and Barbara Broccoli just last year, released the news via the character's official social media accounts. Just before 7 a.m. PT on Monday, a tuxedo-wearing Pattinson emerged from the shadows, gun in hand. His announcement post has accrued over 1.2 million likes on X at the time of writing. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4],[300,251]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories News Over 400 U.K. Industry Figures Demand End to "Culture of Open Secrets" After Russell Brand Trial Movies Tallinn Market Debates Estonia's Film Sector and How Foreign Shoots "Bring Both Value and Tension"

Thanks to Robert Eggers , who directed Pattinson in his 2019 period horror-fantasy The Lighthouse , we now know what the next Bond was up to when that news became public and sent the internet into a tailspin. During an on-camera interview with IGN , Eggers was asked if, while Pattinson's character killed a seagull during the making of The Lighthouse , he ever stopped to consider: &#8220;There goes the next James Bond.&#8221;

&#8220;No, but I'm not surprised that it panned out the way that it did,&#8221; replied the Nosferatu filmmaker. &#8220;And also, crazily enough, he was watching a screening of Werwulf when the news broke, and I was just texting with him about it this morning,&#8221; he said, laughing.

Werwulf , set for release on Dec. 25 in the U.S., stars Eggers regulars Aaron Taylor-Johnson, Lily-Rose Depp, Willem Dafoe and Ralph Ineson, and is set to chronicle a cursed farmer's transformation into a monstrous creature in 13th-century England. Coincidentally, Taylor-Johnson was among the rumored Bond contenders for a long time. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

So what did our new Bond think of Werwulf ? &#8220;Oh, he loved it, of course,&#8221; added Eggers.

Pattinson, who this year alone has starred in The Drama , The Odyssey , Primetime and still has Dune 3 to come, said on Monday through an Amazon MGM Studios press release: &#8220;James Bond is one of the most legendary characters in cinema, and to be entrusted with 007 is the honor of a lifetime. I'm deeply grateful to Denis, Amy, David, Tanya and everyone at Amazon MGM Studios for their belief in me. To say this role is a dream come true is an understatement. I couldn't be more excited about this partnership and the work ahead with this fantastic team.&#8221;

He won a months-long race for the role, beating fellow screen-testers Callum Turner, Josh O'Connor and Jack Lowden. Villeneuve, who recently worked with Pattinson on the final Dune outing, is directing a script from Peaky Blinders mastermind Steven Knight.

The 40-year-old actor is also already the face of DC Studio's Batman . THR took a deep dive into why his casting is t he smart but safe choice as the Bond franchise enters a new era. Fan reaction to the announcement is here &#x2014; and some are suggesting Pattinson's starring role in Twilight predicted his future Bond role .

In fact, Pattinson might have even manifested it . In 2012, during the height of his Twilight fame, the actor was asked in an interview if he could be playing 007 in 10 years' time. &#8220;Yeah, definitely,&#8221; he replied, &#8220;but more like 20 years. [&#8230;] I think there'd be nothing worse than, like, &#x2018;Let's get a fresh-faced Bond!' That would be the worst idea in the world. After Daniel Craig, you have to have some baggage. It would be ridiculous to reinvent [him] as some young posh kid. Or, if [he] was, I'd have to get so tortured in the first few scenes. I'd have to do the first film with one arm or something.&#8221;

Deadline has partnered with The Brit List to profile some of the emerging writers who have made this year's ranking of the best unproduced UK film and TV projects. Launched in 2007, The Brit List has previously featured projects including The King's Speech and Responsible Child . Here, we profile Gemma Addy , who has topped the list with her movie script Dig For Victory .

Gemma Addy harked back to her grandmothers' experiences during World War Two when she set about writing Dig for Victory, which has topped this year's Brit List.

Addy was in the midst of working on a different project about female bootleggers in the 1920s when she came across a photograph of a girl working on a vegetable patch during the war, which captivated her. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article1-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid","mid-articleX","mid-article","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[2,2],[300,250],[620,350],[300,251],[501,282],[3,3],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Watch on Deadline if ( !window.pmc.harmony?.isEventAdScheduledTime() ) { pmcCnx.cmd.push(function() { pmcCnx({ settings: { plugins: { pmcAtlasMG: { iabPlcmt: 2, } } }, playerId: '32fe25c4-79aa-406a-af44-69b41e969e71', playlistId: '0199e506-e642-7728-8264-463efe71337b', }).render("connatix_contextual_player_div"); }); } else { // This should only be get called when page cache is not cleared and it's event time. window.pmc.harmony?.switchToHarmonyPlayer(); } Related Stories News Brit List 2026: Crime Series & Coming-Of-Age Movie Lead List Of Best Unproduced Screenplays News Writer-Performer Jack Holden Channels 'It's A Sin' For Raucous Gay Liberation Movement TV Drama 'Revolting' - Brit List 2025

&#8220;She'd been growing things for the war effort and was holding the most enormous bunch of carrots you've ever seen,&#8221; said Addy. &#8220;They were bigger than she was, and she was smiling at the camera. For whatever reason, when I looked at the photo, I thought, &#8216;Wow, this is probably the best time of your life.' She got to be outside growing vegetables, with all the freedom, independence and recognition that came with it.&#8221;

Addy's attention was grabbed by the picture and she recalled the story of both her grandmothers, one of whom looked after evacuees during the war and the other was a Red Cross nurse in London. &#8220;They always spoke about the war, strange as it sounds, as some of the best times of their lives,&#8221; said Addy. &#8220;It's such a complicated feeling, and it's always intrigued me.&#8221;

Addy therefore reflected on how while men were out fighting, a huge number of women suddenly found themselves joining the workforce. Relinquishing this from 1945 was anything but easy. &#8220;Those closing months were an uneasy time for women,&#8221; said Addy. &#8220;On the one hand, they're deeply relieved that the war is ending; on the other, they're deeply anxious about their own futures.&#8221;

These thoughts helped crystalize Dig for Victory in Addy's mind. The movie is set in rural Wiltshire, UK, as an isolated girl, Edith, comes of age during the closing months of World War Two. When a violent incident interrupts a new friendship with an older woman, Edith uncovers a series of secrets that will shape a deadly future. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dl-mid-article-inject2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article2","mid-articleX","mid-article","mid","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Addy, whose past credits include the BBC and Canal+'s Marie Antoinette and who is working on a project with Scoop producer The Lighthouse, is hoping the Brit list will give the movie a leg up. Dig for Victory received 25 nominations, placing it first alongside Archie Thompson's Petroc. The nominations were unveiled last night at a ceremony in London.

Addy already has ideal cast in mind. While assuming young protagonist Edith would be played by a rising star, Addy's dream for the older woman is the &#8220;charismatic&#8221; and &#8220;captivating&#8221; Indira Varma. For Edith's mother, she settles on Emily Blunt.

Addy thinks the tone she's trying to achieve would be perfect for actors like Varma and Blunt. She was inspired by movies like Florence Pugh-starrer The Wonder and Olivia Colman's Wicked Little Letters, the latter of which is &#8220;darkly comic&#8221; and has a &#8220;main character who felt a little unusual,&#8221; she said. &#8220;I wanted it to be tense and foreboding,&#8221; added Addy. &#8220;Even though the war is at arm's length, there are still planes flying overhead and a sense of threat. I wanted it to feel a little edgy, with twists and turns that keep you on your toes.&#8221;

Addy is hoping she can make something of Dig for Victory but notes it is getting harder for up-and-coming writers to break through, especially since Covid.

&#8220;I heard something quite frightening the other day, which is that they don't really like new writers in rooms at the moment,&#8221; she added. &#8220;They want people who are more established, and that takes a rung off the ladder. But I'm optimistic. I like to think more and more things are getting made.&#8221;

Addy is repped in the UK by The Artists Partnership's Penelope Killick.

Indian music label and film studio T-Series and digital entertainment company Hungama Digital Media Entertainment have teamed up to launch FastTV, a microdrama platform backed by an INR3 billion ($32 million) commitment from the two partners.

The money will fund original programming, technology, and growing and activating its customer base. T-Series and Hungama own the venture in equal shares. Related Stories AI's Hidden Footprint, From Quartz Mines to Kenyan Click-Workers, Bared in Taiwan Creative Content Fest Doc 'The Technological Sublime' AI Could Make Filmmaking Costlier Than the 35mm Era, Korean Pioneers Warn at Taiwan Creative Content Fest

The launch arrives as the short-episode format surges worldwide. According to the companies, microdrama generated $11 billion in global revenue last year and is on track to top $14 billion by the close of 2026. In India, two in three viewers of the format first came across it within the past year. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

FastTV's originals are being written specifically for mobile screens by writers, directors and actors drawn from Indian film and television. The series span multiple genres and languages, with episodes built to be watched in one go and picked up again the following day. The partners plan to adapt the stories into India's many languages and take them to audiences in the 190 countries where Indian content already finds viewers.

Hungama spent the past 18 months designing and building the FastTV platform along with the AI production technology that powers it, for which it has applied for a patent, and is contributing both to the joint venture. The companies said the system lets stories be developed, produced and localized across languages fast enough to keep up with the format, and tailors the viewing experience to each user. T-Series will supply original music and soundtracks for FastTV's series. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#8220;We see immense potential in combining compelling storytelling, world-class music and technology to create a distinctive viewing experience,&#8221; said Bhushan Kumar , chair and managing director of T-Series. &#8220;With FastTV, our endeavor is to develop a strong Indian content ecosystem that can resonate with audiences not just in India, but across the world.&#8221;

T-Series, which runs the most-subscribed music channel on YouTube, has produced more than 180 films, including &#8220;Animal,&#8221; &#8220;Kabir Singh,&#8221; &#8220;Drishyam 2&#8221; and &#8220;Border 2.&#8221; Hungama has run digital platforms spanning music, video, games and immersive media for two decades and says it reaches more than 300 million users. In the last 12 months it has recast itself as an AI-first business with its own AI studio.

&#8220;India does not need another microdrama app. It needs a company built for the way a billion people now watch: stories written for the phone, told in minutes and made to travel,&#8221; said Neeraj Roy, founder and CEO of Hungama.

FastTV's first slate of originals is in development, and programming and genre details will follow in the coming weeks.

Organizing a film festival is a challenge at the best of times, figuring out the logistics with the threat of war on the doorstep requires an extra layer of resilience. It is a quality Fatma Al Remaihi, CEO of the Doha Film Institute (DFI) and her team have had to muster in spades as they prepared for the second edition of the rebooted Doha Film Festival , setting in place logistics for a trio of different scenarios. "We knew that no matter what, we will have a festival&#x2026; we were exploring differing scenarios, that it could be just for locals, that it could be for everyone, or that it could just be an online competition. We've planned for every eventuality," Al Remaihi tells Deadline on the eve of the festival which is due to open on Thursday. She is speaking nine months after Israel and the U.S. launched a major military campaign against Iran's Islamic Republic government at the end of February, which retaliated with missile and drone strike on all its Gulf neighbours. The DFI was forced to move its popular annual Qumra project and talent incubator online in March, with the team applying lessons learned during the Covid-19 pandemic. While guests, including Gael Garc&#xED;a Bernal and Diego Luna and Alice Diop, could not make it to Qatar , Al Remaihi says the event still fulfilled its mission. "The filmmakers and the projects didn't miss this edition. It was fantastic to be able to be there for them. Even though a lot of our staff here were suffering with scary times, they're still pulled through and made it happen for the filmmakers." The situation has calmed down since April and Qatar has been heavily involved in negotiations to deescalate the conflict, with its diplomats regularly traveling to the Iranian capital Tehran as part of international efforts to broker a peace deal. Al Remaihi is confident that the festival, running from November 19 to 27, will unfold at full capacity, alongside other events set to take place in Doha in the period including the Fashion Trust Arabia Awards on November 28 and the Formula 1 Grand Prix from November 27 to 28. "We're so happy that we are a few days away from the festival, and excited now to open and experience it with everybody, and it's not smaller, we have more films and more guests," she says. The festival expands on the DFI's long-running family-focused festival of Ajyal which ran for more than a decade during the same period, with Media City Qatar and the territory's Film Committee coming on board as new partners as part of their mission to build the country's media and entertainment sector. "I don't like to call it a second edition as I don't see it as a new festival. It's a continuation of what we do," said Al Remaihi, while acknowledging that the repositioning gave the festival fresh impetus last year. The first edition of the reboot last year set the tone for a festival focused on cinema and rather than big name stars. "The guests that we invite are from the program. That's what is important for us. They have a role in the program and something to give in the festival. It's never been about big names and stars for us," says Al Remaihi. She points nonetheless to the presence of Japanese director Naomi Kawase as jury president for the main International Competition; James Gray , for a gala screening of Paper Tiger , and Oscar-winning documentarian Gianfranco Rosi ,who is also on the jury alongside rising actor Adam Bessa, Tunisian producer Dora Bouchoucha and Palestinian director Kamal Aljafari. Related Stories News Festival In Focus: Marrakech's Atlas Workshops Nurtures Bespoke Approach As Project Incubator Goes From Strength-To-Strength News Festival In Focus: Marrakech Navigates Turbulent World To Celebrate Established Cinema Artists & Newcomers

Indian filmmaker and actor Nandita Das ( Manto , Zwigato ) and regional star, the Syrian actor Taim Hassan ( Al Hayba , Mawlana ), will also be in attendance to receive the festival's Creative Excellence Award. "We have big names. Maybe for the public eyes, it's different. But for people who work in the industry and who value filmmaking and the craft itself, they know these names and they're very excited to have them in Doha. In that sense, I'm very happy with the caliber of the guests who are coming to the festival," she explains. Watch on Deadline if ( !window.pmc.harmony?.isEventAdScheduledTime() ) { pmcCnx.cmd.push(function() { pmcCnx({ settings: { plugins: { pmcAtlasMG: { iabPlcmt: 2, } } }, playerId: '32fe25c4-79aa-406a-af44-69b41e969e71', playlistId: '0199e506-e642-7728-8264-463efe71337b', }).render("connatix_contextual_player_div"); }); } else { // This should only be get called when page cache is not cleared and it's event time. window.pmc.harmony?.switchToHarmonyPlayer(); }

DFI-backed Yemen-set drama The Station by Sara Ishaq &#x2013; set against the backdrop of woman-only petrol stations in the war-torn country &#x2013; opens this edition. "We confirmed The Station for the opening months ago. It's very rare to have an opening film confirmed from that early, but we saw in this film everything that embodies the festival and the spirit of the festival," says Al Remaihi. "It's a simple story that sheds light on a very complicated background, and we thought it was very brave to make a film like this. We don't see a lot coming out of Yemen. Yes, it had its premiere in Cannes, but we wanted to give it center stage in the festival." The Station is among 15 films playing in the International Competition at the heart of the festival, competing for close to $200,000 in combined prize money. It features a raft of titles which have garnered acclaim on the festival circuit over the past year including Cameroonian director Auguste Bernard Kouemo's House of the Wind , which won Venice's Lion of the Future for a Debut Film when it premiered there in its Orizzonti sidebar in September. Further contenders include Moroccan director Tala Hadid's Bardi , which played at the New York Film Festival, and Thai filmmaker Sompot Chidgasornpongse's 9 Temples to Heaven , which premiered in Cannes Directors' Fortnight and is Thailand's submission to the 99th Academy Awards. Other Oscar hopefuls in the lineup include the Philippines' entry Filipi&#xF1;ana by Rafael Manuel and Chilean director Manuela Martelli's The Meltdown . There are four films by Palestinian filmmakers including Rabab Khamis' documentary Sama , about a ten-year-old girl who collects plastic and cardboard in Deir al-Balah in Gaza to sell as fuel and help sustain her family, which world premieres It is joined by Hosam Abu Dan's Gaza-shot drama Thirteen in Gaza , about a 13-year-old living with her father in a Gaza displacement camp, which won the NETPAC Award in Toronto; Muayad Alayan's Venice&#x2013;selected Conversation with the Sea , about a Palestinian man caught in the crosshairs of Israeli bureaucracy, and Mohanad Yaqubi's Revolutionaries Never Die , revolving around 113 reels of films preserved by Lebanese filmmaker Jocelyne Saab. The final contenders are Asmahan Bkerat's Concrete Land, Hugo Salvaterra's My Semba, Zou Jing Cannes Critics' Week Fipresci-winner A Girl Unknown , Alessandra Sanguinetti's The Illusion of an Everlasting Summer and Alexander Murphy's Tin Castle . Eleven of the films have received the support of the DFI's grantee programs, with Al Remaihi explaining it is at the heart of an interconnected process developed over the institute's 16-year existence. "It just shows that we don't do anything on a singular basis. Everything we do is connected. We have an ecosystem that we built a long time ago, and we've seen the benefits of having something like this," she says. &#8220;A lot of the films in our competition are grantees because it speaks to the type of funding that we do, choices that we make, the people that we work with, and we love to celebrate all of this during the festival with all of that community. It's a great moment for us also to have all of that come together.&#8221; Outside of the competition, there will be gala screening for Paper Tige r, Rami Kodeih's DFI-backed Beirut-set heist thriller Furies, Maiken Baird's Toronto-selected Edward Said: Between Worlds and Bruno Dumont's Red Rocks . blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article1-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid","mid-articleX","mid-article","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[2,2],[300,250],[620,350],[300,251],[501,282],[3,3],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

There will also be Special Screenings for Abderrahmane Sissako's Artou , Daniele Rugo's Life Support , following international doctors working alongside local medical staff in Gaza in 2023; Ginevra Elkann drama The Roses of Marrakech and Ali El Arabi's 52 Blue .

Another key strand is the Made In Qatar sidebar. This has traditionally showcased short films but makes history this year as it premieres f our locally produced features , spanning Jodari Meno by Jamal Al Khanji, A Very Ordinary Life by Mohamed Deniz, First to Fly by Rodrigo Vazquez Salessi and When the News Breaks You by Hamad Al Hajri.

&#8220;It's a testament to all of the work that we've been doing over the years and how the industry has developed in Qatar. We've been doing this for a long time, but now its not just the Doha Film Institute. We the Film Committee, Media City Qatar, and Katara Studios, all the film production houses,&#8221; she says. &#8220;You feel that there is a huge movement and a huge shift in the industry here.&#8221; A new addition to the program is the Cinema Alkhaleej sidebar showcasing cinema from across the Gulf region, with highlights including the world premiere of horror feature Soulala by Saudi director and actor Hisham Fqih as well as the Qatar premiere of Ahd Kamel's My Driver and I , which originally premiered at Saudi Arabia's Red Sea International Film Festival in 2024. "We wanted to bring the Khaleeji (Gulf) industry together and have them more involved in the festival, not just as guests but also as participating guests. A lot of these films were not available here, so they are still fresh for our audiences. I loved watching all these films as we programmed the section and I'm excited to share them with the audience here.&#8221;

John Malkovich and Rosalind Eleazar will headline &#8220; History ,&#8221; a new play by Rebecca Reid, at London's Wyndham's Theatre from May 17 to Aug. 7, 2027.

The play follows Wren, whose creative life has faded since she had a baby. She takes a reckless step by inviting her ex, James, to lunch. He once defined her, and the meeting shows that a chapter she considered closed remains open.

Matthew Dunster directs the limited run, which is Reid's first West End production as a playwright. Fiery Angel and Kater Gordon are producing, and further casting has yet to be revealed. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Taiwan Creative Content Fest Opens as Chair Sue Wang Says 'Culture Is Power' Amid AI Shift AI's Hidden Footprint, From Quartz Mines to Kenyan Click-Workers, Bared in Taiwan Creative Content Fest Doc 'The Technological Sublime'

Malkovich, a two-time Academy Award nominee who has also been nominated for a Golden Globe and a BAFTA, currently appears in Martin McDonagh's &#8220;Wild Horse Nine,&#8221; a Searchlight Pictures release. He is a founding member of Chicago's Steppenwolf Theatre Company.

Malkovich said: &#8220;I couldn't be more delighted to start work in the months to come on this fascinating new play, opposite Rosalind Eleazar and directed by Matthew Dunster.&#8221;

Eleazar plays Louisa Guy in Apple TV+'s &#8220;Slow Horses&#8221; and stars in the title role of A24's &#8220;Misty Green.&#8221; That performance has drawn honors from several festivals, including the Toronto Film Festival and the Hamptons International Film Festival. Her stage credits include &#8220;Small Hotel&#8221; alongside Ralph Fiennes at Theatre Royal Bath, and an earlier engagement at Wyndham's Theatre in &#8220;The Starry Messenger,&#8221; where she appeared with Matthew Broderick.

Eleazar said: &#8220;The prospect of working with John has long been a dream of mine and so bringing this story to life with this team is super exciting.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Reid is an author, screenwriter and journalist whose six novels include &#8220;Perfect Liars,&#8221; &#8220;Truth Hurts&#8221; and &#8220;Two Wrongs.&#8221; Her stage adaptation of &#8220;Single White Female&#8221; recently completed a half-year ATG tour.

Reid said: &#8220;It's a funny, sexy and provocative story about marriage, power, escapism, motherhood and making bad, selfish, delicious choices.&#8221;

Dunster, a four-time Olivier nominee, has directed or written more than 60 theater productions. His Broadway production of &#8220;Hangmen&#8221; earned five Tony nominations, and his credits include &#8220;The Hunger Games on Stage&#8221; and &#8220;2:22 &#x2013; A Ghost Story,&#8221; whose West End run reached 694 performances. Gordon, an Emmy winner for her writing on &#8220;Mad Men,&#8221; also helped bring &#8220;2:22 &#x2013; A Ghost Story&#8221; to the stage.

Dunster said: &#8220;John brings the heft of greatness. Rosalind is electrifying on stage and screen.&#8221;

EXCLUSIVE: Ananya Panday is nearly ready to return as India &#8216;s main Bae.

Prime Video has a set December 17 as the launch date of Call Me Bae Season 2, in which Panday leads an ensemble cast as wealthy social influencer Bella Chowdhary, or Bae, whose messy love life is the center of the storyline.

It has been a long wait for the second season of the chaotic comedy romance series, with Season 2 getting the greenlight back in September 2024. Season 1 was widely viewed in India and featured among the Top 10 trending titles on Prime Video in more than 50 countries, including the U.S., UK, Canada, Australia, U.A.E., Saudi Arabia, Malaysia, New Zealand and South Africa. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article1-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid","mid-articleX","mid-article","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[2,2],[300,250],[620,350],[300,251],[501,282],[3,3],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories News E. Niwas Returns To Feature Directing With Supernatural Comedy-Adventure 'Kurla Boyz' From Abundantia Entertainment & One Entertainment Films News 'Beast Games' Sets Season 3 Release Date At Prime Video

